one of the things we've been trying to  understand in this cost of living crisis  is just how much different energy costs  because you know primarily it's natural  gas that's gone up a lot in price and so  has electricity how that kind of feeds  down the chain and what we've done is to  try to start the very start of the chain  [Music]  now you ever wondered where salt comes  from table salt well the answer is  actually 400 meters beneath our feet  here in picturesque cheshire  there is a very big slab of salt down in  the ground and under here there is  what's called a solution mine how does  it work well basically compressed air  goes through that pipe and then you've  got water pumped in through that pipe  that goes down into this enormous cavern  that's basically got water going through  it so you're mining rather than mining  with pickaxes you're mining with water  and then out of this pipe comes  have a look at this  that is brine  so that is very salty water that's  coming directly out of the ground  this is incredibly salty actually  and that's kind of the beginning of a  process  that  tells you a few things it tells you  about how industry works around this  country because that is the start of so  many different supply chains whether  it's in pharmaceuticals whether it's in  food whether there's all sorts of other  things as well  but also  it's an energy intensive process this  gets piped off around the countryside it  gets turned in salt gets turned into  other products but a lot of gas is spent  along the way trying to process it  and understanding that supply chain  understanding how we get this seemingly  simple material  tells you quite a lot about this cost of  living crisis we're going through right  now  [Music]  we are at the nippon electric class  factory just outside wigan  and  i'm willing to bet that you might not  have thought all that much about  fiberglass i haven't really but right  now having just been through this  process and seen how it's made i can  tell you it's one of the most  extraordinary things ever  uh we'll get to what it's for in a  moment but basically it's for a lot of  important things  what's striking about this and we've  watched as it's gone through a furnace  you've seen sand and other ingredients  melted down at a  5 600 degrees see them turned into  basically kind of molten glass we've  seen them threaded into tiny  tiny strands thinner than a human hair  and then wound up again  and a few things have left out first of  all  this is an incredibly energy intensive  process  this place does it pretty much better  than most other places in the world but  it takes a lot of energy it takes a lot  of natural gas it takes a lot of  electricity to power the furnaces and  then you've got these kind of big  drawing uh equipment over here where  it's getting blasted with hot air well  that's  both drying it but at the same time it's  curing it so it has a kind of double uh  function  and there is no simple way of just  replacing one of these different  things they do and energy sources with  electricity and i think that's one of  the important things we're starting to  learn here is as we try and work out how  to become less reliant on natural gas  specifically maybe in this case russian  natural gas  there are so many different processes in  how we get the stuff we need which need  natural gas at least right now we have  no proven way of replacing them with  electricity for the time being at least  but the end product is this this is  fiberglass and you can see it's just  so essentially strands and it's in coils  here but what is that for that is going  to become part of the wind turbines that  are going to generate the new green  electricity we're going to have in the  future and in order to get that you need  natural gas and therein lies the big  paradox that we're facing at the moment  we need these fossil fuels to build the  stuff  to help us escape  from the fossil fuels  this is valley growing nurseries we're  just outside of london and this is an  enormous greenhouse devoted to well  growing tomatoes and if you want to grow  this stuff here  in the uk there's a whole load of  different costs so energy you need to  try and keep this greenhouse at a kind  of regular temperature just over 20 20  21 degrees and as well as that you need  to have it kind of stocked with extra  carbon dioxide one of the clever things  they do here is they've got a natural  gas boiler that's kind of doing the  heating and those bars on the on the  floor there are providing the heating  but as well as providing heating the  natural gas boiler also has excess co2  the stuff that goes normally into the  atmosphere well that's pumped into here  and then  aids photosynthesis so tomatoes in  particular they like a lot of co2 so in  here  the parts per million of co2 is actually  kind of two three times what it is in  the atmosphere  outside you put all of that together and  you're talking about a lot of energy  costs that need to go into production  and that's not the only thing you've  also got fertilizer costs they've gone  up by multiples as well why because  fertilizer is made in part from natural  gas and it's a very energy intensive  process as well  put all of that together and it helps  explain why costs here are up by 40 or  so maybe even more  and helps explain why food prices in the  coming  months could well get even more  expensive than they are at the moment  that's a bit of an issue given we're all  facing a cost of living crisis and given  that food costs  are one of the things that affects  particularly those who are on lower  incomes even more than others all of  which is why we need to understand and  think about how we make the foods we eat  and what those higher energy costs are  doing to them
 

